WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.640
Recent vaccine trial results have been cause for&nbsp;
optimism that we can combat the COVID-19 pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.760
But how confident can we be in these results?
Pharmaceutical company Pfizer announced that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:22.160
vaccine is 95% effective. This result is based off&nbsp;
of clinical trials that had 43,000 participants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.920
But what happens when this vaccine is given to&nbsp;
the broader population? Well, based on statistics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.120
we can conclude that there is a 99 in 100 chance&nbsp;
that the Pfizer vaccine has a true efficacy rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:40.560
above 90%. What does that mean? A 95% efficacy rate&nbsp;
means that one out of every 21 positive cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.880
in the trial are in the vaccinated group. If we&nbsp;
apply these odds independently to each of the 170&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.560
positive cases in the trial, there is a 1 in 100&nbsp;
chance of observing 15 or more vaccinated cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:59.680
which is the same as a 90% efficacy rate. After a&nbsp;
long battle against COVID-19, the end is in sight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.120
Continuing to gather data is a must, but&nbsp;
vaccine optimism is certainly not misplaced.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.960
Would you take a 95% effective&nbsp;
vaccine? Let us know in our poll.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.200
Also, let us know what other topics you'd&nbsp;
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.880
and be sure to like and subscribe&nbsp;
for more research and videos from AEI.

